# Harnesses Are Just Brain Tricks

The chronology of harness engineering shares a lot with that of human development. Here are some problems that both humans and agents experience, and their respective solutions:

               Problem       Human solution       Agent solution                       Cannot effectively review one's own work       Peer review       Spin up another agent to review                          Long problems are difficult              Notetaking       Agents write things down                 Big problems are difficult       Organizations                Subagents                                 Hyperspecific workflows              Training       Skills                          Some actions are dangerous              Security                Sandboxing                                 Getting lost in the weeds              Set a goal                Set a /goal                        Slow, repetitive tasks       Scripting       Code mode         

It's hard not to say the human and agent solutions look alike. I think you can reasonably predict future harness improvements in the same way:

               Problem       Human solution       Predicted agent solution                                Chain of command is slow              DMs                Talk across subagent trees                                 Stepping on toes              Meetings/Slack                Targeted and temporary agent chatrooms                                 Burnout              Time off                Being run with no task                                 Tribal knowledge discovery              Ask for help                Create and share skills with other agents                                 Lack of trust              Reputation, gossip                Create and share evaluations of agents with other agents                                 Good behavior is hard to define and always changing              Natural selection                Run experiments on altered copies of oneself,         keeping the winners                

Some of these are extreme, but so were the ones in the first table. In fact in the time since I started thinking about this topic, several rows moved from the second table to the first.

I have to admit that even though it makes sense that this pattern would hold, I still find myself dumbfounded in the ways that it does. They somehow seem obvious in retrospect, but surprising in how minimal the right solution actually turned out to be—both for human development and harness engineering.
